# Statement on Fire Accidents under Rule 82
Tamil title: 82ஆவது விதியின்கீழ் தீ விபத்துகள்பற்றிய அறிக்கை

Speech 21, 8 July 1967

**Honourable C. N. Annadurai:** Mr Speaker, I wish to inform the House about the series of fires occurring in Madras city and the problems arising from them. The fires have now largely been brought under control. During the first two days, the panic that arose immediately after the outbreaks truly disrupted people's everyday lives. The Government's measures to prevent fires and reduce their extent have begun to produce results.

The scale first fell from two hundred huts destroyed to one hundred and then fifty. Now, whenever a hut catches fire, it is detected immediately and measures to prevent its spread are succeeding. During the past two or three days, fires in a few scattered huts were extinguished at once and major accidents prevented.

I do not say that fires have stopped completely or that the public's fear has disappeared. No one can see ordinary people, especially hut dwellers, subjected to this horror without tears. Even where their huts have not burned, many are living by the roadside because they fear what may happen if fire breaks out while they sleep. They have placed all their possessions beside the road and watch anxiously for danger to their huts. It is a heart-rending sight.

To prevent this condition from continuing, police and Home Guards are maintaining extensive patrols in poor settlements and vigorously seeking suspicious persons. Senior police officers patrol these localities and work to reduce panic. Volunteers experienced in public service are offering reassurance.

Municipal schools have been opened as temporary shelters for everyone who has lost a home, and the schools have consequently been closed for two or three days. For six or seven days the Corporation has accepted responsibility for food packets, preparing meals in the kitchens established for the midday-meal scheme. Members of the public, charitable organisations and the Red Cross are also providing assistance.

Relief work will continue, and the Government will accelerate it and create permanent arrangements to prevent these people becoming victims again. Anyone who says the Government failed to rescue, protect, or provide immediate relief is merely continuing a pre-existing opinion of us. The necessary work to restore conditions is being done.

The Government has decided to raise substantially the relief formerly paid to people who lost homes through natural calamity or fire. A household that has lost its hut and clothing will now receive Rs 80; the earlier amounts were Rs 40 and Rs 10. The Corporation also pays Rs 25 for each home.

Knowing that these sums alone cannot solve the problem, the Government has formed a special committee of leaders from every party to provide suitable relief. The leaders generously asked me to chair it. In a few moments I shall explain the means proposed not only for immediate help but for a permanent remedy.

First, successive fires and widespread suspicions have caused some frightened residents to patrol hut areas with sticks and weapons and commit undesirable, condemnable and hateful acts. I was deeply distressed to read that my friend and Upper House member T. G. Krishnamurthi was attacked yesterday.

Senior police officers have been told that no explanation will excuse the attack, whoever committed it and whatever motive they claim. Saying that they were on guard and struck someone they suspected will not be accepted. Action may be taken against everyone involved. Some may already have been arrested and released on bail. The police have been instructed to arrest those engaging in such conduct.

I warn both the public and people claiming to serve them, even genuine volunteers, not to exceed their role when police officers are present. They must not seize and beat anyone they happen to see or descend into rowdyism. If this condition grows, people will use mere suspicion as a pretext for disorder and violence. Newspapers should publicise this warning so that sound confidence may be restored.

Whenever disasters occur, wrongdoers naturally attempt to exploit the situation. This Government neither wants nor will allow such conduct to grow, and action has been taken.

Rumours about who causes the fires have made people suspicious of everyone. If someone merely looks closely at a hut, residents fear he intends to burn it. I assure the public that they need not yield even slightly to such panic. Police, Home Guards and welfare volunteers are watchful. If any conspiracy exists, they are working energetically to discover and defeat it. Whatever the plot, it will be found and thwarted.

People should therefore set aside their anxiety and trust that the Government is acting to prevent further outbreaks. At the same time, the Government will not permit people claiming to keep watch to commit hateful acts.

Many members have spoken of a permanent remedy. We cannot say that Madras city's huts stand in planned locations or follow a planned layout. The former Government did not make provision for this; it repeatedly tried to remove settlements that sprang up suddenly. But people arriving from outside in search of work, with nowhere else to shelter, built wherever they found an open space. In this manner roughly six hundred hut settlements arose in the city.

The huts within them were not laid out systematically. In an emergency even ten rescuers may be unable to enter, because no access route exists, and rescue work is obstructed.

While rebuilding fire-damaged homes, we must therefore reorganise the sites and improve the burned areas. New dwellings should not again be thatched wherever possible, but roofed with asbestos or zinc sheets. Yesterday's committee decided that layouts and materials should limit major damage even if a fire begins accidentally.

This requires reorganising the existing hut settlements across Madras. The Government has decided to carry it out through a planned four-year programme and seeks the cooperation of every party and the public. No government can solve the problem if, while old thatched settlements are being reorganised on one side, new huts arise on the other.

Members of every party, respected non-party public figures, Corporation representatives and others must unite in reorganising the existing huts and explaining to the public that no new huts can be allowed in Madras. Yesterday's all-party committee resolved to make that commitment.

I must reveal a political secret. Ten people put up huts somewhere; when an order is issued to separate or remove them, they bring a legislator or municipal councillor and ask for ten more days. Soon they become impossible to remove. Hut dwellers can know more political strategy than politicians. They hang a board bearing the names of leaders of whichever party governs, then claim affection and rights through those leaders. Some affection may be genuine, but difficulty begins when it becomes pressure.

Boards that bore other names four months ago have now been changed to ours, declaring the places our “kingdoms.” No party wants such a kingdom; it brings neither gain nor benefit. Whether the board reads Kamarajapuram, Karuthirumanpuram or Karunanidhipettai, politicians must decline invitations to obstruct reorganisation. Then the effort to improve all settlements within four or five years can succeed. Opposition leaders themselves proposed that the committee meet regularly.

People who have just lost homes immediately need food, clothing, medicine when ill, and shelter. Money is already being spent, but much more is required. While every settlement may be reorganised over four or five years, the committee decided to rebuild the presently burned areas at once in the new manner.

The Government pays Rs 80 and the Corporation Rs 25 per hut. Experts say a fire-resistant replacement will cost Rs 400 to Rs 450. If experts estimate Rs 450, direct organisation may bring it near Rs 400, but it cannot be done for less. The committee therefore appealed to public-spirited people, those with means, industrialists and workers to supply the balance after the Government allocation.

The OCR donation ledger records the following readable contributions: Rs 10,000 from the relief fund opened in the Chief Minister's name; Rs 5,900 from the DMK fund; Rs 5,000 from *The Hindu*; Rs 1,000 from S. S. Vasan of *Ananda Vikatan*; Rs 1,001 from the State Bank of India Staff Union; Rs 5,000 each from *Dinamani*, the *Indian Express* and *Dina Thanthi*; Rs 10,000 from Madras Corporation Limited; Rs 25,000 from the Madras Race Club; Rs 501 from an Indian Bank directors' body; Rs 129 from an engineering-works cyclone-relief committee; Rs 107 from Harijan Welfare Department officers; Rs 1,000 from Sri Krishna Textiles; Rs 675 from the Bengal Association; Rs 3,000 from the Nilgiri Bus Owners' Association; Rs 2,000 from *Malai Murasu*; and Rs 7,050 from DMK legislators. Several donor names and amounts in Images 8–9 are damaged and require checking against the scans.

Industrialists who met me yesterday evening promised substantial further sums: the readable ledger includes Rs 25,000 from India Cements, Rs 10,000 from Industrial Chemicals, Rs 2,500 from TVS, Rs 10,000 from Amalgamations Limited, and Rs 30,000 from Marudai Pillai. I falter slightly while reading these sums because I have neither seen nor handled such large amounts before! (Laughter.)

The Leader of the Opposition, Mr Karuthiruman, gives Rs 251; K. Vinayagam gives one month's salary; Electricity Department employees gave Rs 108; and the Madras Round Table Association gave Rs 1,000. Above all, I am happy to report that a telephone message from Delhi says the Prime Minister of India has contributed Rs 50,000.

The committee will use these donations to carry out the relief work described. Work in the places already burned is estimated to cost nearly Rs 5 lakh. Because every hut settlement in Madras must ultimately be reorganised, the committee resolved to become a permanent “City Fire Relief and Rehabilitation Committee” and continue the effort. I thank the newspapers for their support and goodwill.

Almost every newspaper has expressed sympathy and offered proposals in editorials. Proprietors have told me they will set aside space and collect funds. I intend to remain in contact with them.

As a first stage, I hope to raise Rs 1 crore and transform the areas called slums and hut settlements so they cease to be places exposed to such suffering. I ask the public, newspapers, welfare volunteers, political parties, party leaders, legislators and everyone else to work together in a way that strengthens this purpose.

Existing settlements give foreign visitors a poor impression of our city and administration. More importantly, sudden disasters cost lives: children cry in anguish, women panic, and thousands lose homes, happiness, possessions, livelihoods and peace of mind. This condition must be removed.

I neither want nor support political interference in this work. As I ask everyone to cooperate, I state categorically that the law will act without the slightest leniency against anyone who, while claiming to protect fire victims, engages in antisocial conduct, rowdyism, incitement or assault.

No one should say, “Someone was beaten and they remain idle.” No person in this country should regard another as merely “someone.” We were born to live together and are bound to serve the public together. In times of political doubt, panic, accident and disorder, we must not create conditions that hostile forces can exploit. Every political party must be united in this.

Krishnamurthi's assault was reported in the press and mentioned by many friends. What matters is not simply that a Congressman was beaten; he was once even an opponent of Congress. What matters is that people claiming to protect the public blindly and violently assaulted a man going about his work. Both this Government and I personally accept that it was condemnable, hateful and must be stopped. The police will act energetically in that spirit.

It was a matter of pride that representatives of every party attended yesterday to consider the details, funds already collected, further collection and proper decisions. The Leader of the Opposition had walked out the previous day over a difference of opinion. Many told me he would not attend; I said they should not think so. He came and offered suggestions.

Communists, Socialists, the Tamil Arasu Kazhagam leader, the Muslim League leader and the Republican Party leader all showed concern. I gratefully accept the opportunity to continue working through the committee.

Though many departments are allotted to me as Chief Minister, I shall give this work first and foremost importance. I had hoped, and still hope, to devote a large part of my thought and energy to reorganising huts and helping the many thousands of poor people who suffer when fires or natural calamities occur. I ask everyone to cooperate in implementing that hope and the committee's decision. Mr Speaker, this is the position in Madras city that I wished to report to the House.

**Annadurai, in a further reply:** As the Leader of the Opposition said, the rowdyism of people who claim to serve the frightened public will be suppressed. The Government accepts responsibility for giving them no room.

He said people feel disgust merely on seeing khadi. My own shirt looks like khadi, though it is not. Showing such revulsion is a sign of barbarism. I have not the slightest desire to let it grow.

---NOTES---
The source identifies this as Speech 21, an Assembly statement made under Rule 82 on 8 July 1967 concerning repeated fires in Madras hut settlements.

The Tamil OCR source is preserved verbatim in the bilingual output. Several lines in the donation ledger across Images 8 and 9 are damaged; only clearly recoverable donor-amount pairs are translated, and all amounts require scan verification before financial citation.

“குடிசைப் பகுதிகள்” is rendered as hut settlements, while “சேரிப் பகுதிகள்” is rendered contextually as slum areas. Both are historical administrative descriptions in the speech.

The boards named Kamarajapuram, Karuthirumanpuram and Karunanidhipettai describe political patronage used to resist settlement regulation; “kingdoms” preserves Annadurai's irony.

The source proposes asbestos or zinc-sheet roofing according to 1967 practice. Translation preserves the historical proposal and does not imply present-day safety endorsement.
